#!/bin/sh
# Refuse a commit message that would close the reference it only meant to name.
#
# Why this exists. On 2026-10-16 a documentation commit on `main` carried the
# line "both are merged and resolved: #18, where both sides appended an item".
# It was prose about a pull request. GitHub read `resolved: #18` as an
# instruction, closed pull request #18 -- four answered review rounds -- and
# attributed the act to whoever pushed, so the timeline accused a person while
# the cause sat in a commit message nobody had reason to re-read. It took three
# wrong readings to find, and the `closed` event had carried the commit id all
# along.
#
# What GitHub actually does: `close`, `fix` and `resolve`, in every form, with
# an optional colon, applied to the reference that follows -- `#12`, `GH-12`,
# `owner/repo#12`, or a full issue or pull URL -- and it applies them to pull
# requests as well as issues. Only commit messages on the default branch and
# pull request descriptions trigger it; ordinary comments do not.
#
# A commit may of course mean to close an issue. That is what the override is
# for: a guard that forbids a real workflow gets worked around rather than used.
set -u

msg_file="$1"

# Git's own comment lines, and anything below the verbose-commit scissors, are
# not part of the message the forge reads.
body=$(sed -e '/^#/d' -e '/^------------------------ >8 /,$d' "$msg_file")

keywords='(close|closes|closed|fix|fixes|fixed|resolve|resolves|resolved)'
ref='(#|[Gg][Hh]-|[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+#)[0-9]+'
url='https?://github\.com/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/(issues|pull)/[0-9]+'

hits=$(printf '%s\n' "$body" | grep -niE "${keywords}[[:space:]]*:?[[:space:]]*(${ref}|${url})" || true)

[ -n "$hits" ] || exit 0

if [ "${ALLOW_CLOSING_KEYWORD:-0}" = "1" ]; then
  printf 'commit-msg: closing keyword allowed by ALLOW_CLOSING_KEYWORD=1:\n%s\n' "$hits" >&2
  exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<MSG

commit-msg refused this message: it would close the reference it names.

$hits

GitHub reads close, fix and resolve -- in any form, with or without a colon --
as an instruction to close whatever reference follows, on pull requests as well
as issues, and it names whoever pushes as the actor rather than the commit. A
sentence that merely talks about a pull request closes it.

To describe one, put a word between the verb and the number: "the #18 conflict",
"on #18", "answered on #18". To close an issue on purpose:

    ALLOW_CLOSING_KEYWORD=1 git commit ...

The message is kept in .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG; nothing was committed.
MSG
exit 1
